    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n43790" root="&#xA;                        ميل&#xA;                    " body-key="m1">
                        
                            <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">امالةُ الأَلِفِ</span></b> 
                        
                        <i>The inclining of the sound of</i>
                        <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ا</span>
                        <i>when quiescent, after fet-hah, towards the sound
of</i>
                        <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ى</span>; <i>so that the fet-hah, with that</i>
                        <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ا</span>, <i>composes
a sound the same as that of the long</i> “ e ” <i>in the
English word</i> “ <i>there.</i> ” This is accordant with
present usage; and I have not found any learned
Arab who asserts otherwise. See also <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">نَابٌ</span>, and
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">حَجَّاجٌ</span>, and <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">مَشُوبٌ</span>.

